<section id="names" class="builder">
  <h2>For the builder · a name clash to avoid</h2>
  <p><b><code>SHIP.career</code>, the save's <code>ship.career</code>, <code>CAREER</code> and the <code>career…</code> functions (<code>careerInit</code>, <code>careerOn</code>, <code>careerClockedIn</code>…) already belong to the leave, missed-shift and time-clock system</b> (the career engineer's 0.61.0-0.61.19, in section 20). The plan's <code>save.career</code> and <code>SHIP.career.tools</code> become <b><code>SHIP.works</code> / <code>ship.works</code></b> and <code>SHIP.works.tools</code>; new functions start with <code>works…</code>, <code>ladder…</code> or <code>tool…</code>. <code>CAREER_LADDER</code> and <code>careerSpeed</code> keep the plan's names (neither exists). To the lead: Pillar 5's seat C ("career engineer", <code>claude/works-career</code>) is a different seat from the 0.61.x career engineer (<code>claude/leave</code>); seat C is called the <b>Works career engineer</b> in briefs (the lead, 2026-10-18).</p>
</section>

<section id="faq">
  <h2>Builders' questions</h2>
  <h3>How does a sub-block add a tab to the Works app? (seat H, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>24 core exposes <code>worksTabAdd({ id, title, order, view, act })</code>; the <code>works</code> app shows its own tabs (Training, Work log, Tools) and every added tab in <code>order</code>. Sub-blocks call it typeof-guarded at load. Before 24 core is in a build, 24k registers a stand-in app <code>deviceApp({ id: 'faultnotes', title: 'Fault notes', icon: 'wrench', order: 32, ... })</code>, hidden while no case kind is open; once <code>worksTabAdd</code> exists, 24k uses it and doesn't register the stand-in.</p>
  <h3>Torres is off on Day 5 (seat C, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>Torres's rota is <code>offDays [3, 4]</code> (Thursday and Friday), so he can't teach on Day 5. Rows take a <code>by</code> list, first on shift wins: <code>m7</code> and <code>p3</code> are <code>['torres-zeke', 'halloran-emeka', 'calder']</code>, so <b>Emeka Halloran</b> (a Works systems officer on the day shift, off Saturday and Sunday) teaches both on Day 5, handing over Torres's old scanner, which he left with a note; Calder only if neither is on shift (the lead: a Works officer first, Torres always first when he's on). No rota change; the other rows' teachers are on shift on their days (checked: Torres Tue and Mon, Yara Wed, Brennan and Tomas Thu, Calder Mon-Fri). <code>m2</code>'s "oily job" is <code>flag('works.oily')</code>, set by 24b when you turn in an oily job on Day 8 or later.</p>
  <h3>The work-step field is <code>how</code>, not <code>step</code> (seat S, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>A stop's <code>step</code> already carries its tag ('fetch', 'make', 'job', 'pickup', 'drop', 'cart', 'load', 'unload', 'return'…), read by 15, 15b and onboarding and sent by <code>jobNote</code> as <code>tag</code>. So the kind of work step is <b><code>how: 'hold' | 'look' | 'carry' | 'choose' | 'turn' | 'wait'</code></b> (default <code>'hold'</code>), with <code>opts</code>. Every page now writes <code>how</code>.</p>
  <h3>More real faults: what's built instead of <code>FIND_LATE</code> (seat S, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>A worn machine takes 2-4 days to wear on to faulty, so finding it later can't give 40-60% repairs without leaving the first week with no faults and empty board hours (10 hours late: 0%; 3 days late with 70% of them: 52%, but no faults before Day 5). <b>Built instead (agreed):</b> from Day 2, on your shift days, about half of the worn machines at your tier (picked with <code>schedU(machSeed(id), day, 856)</code>) are found 2-24 hours later already at the top of faulty (condition 49), as a <code>repair</code> with a cause. The story is the same ("the crew didn't catch it while it was only worn"). Limits (as built): never a machine with a held reserved job or an open head-scratcher case; the drop is <code>min(cond, 49)</code>, once; only on days where Day ≥ 2, <code>playerShiftOn</code>, your tier and not the engine kinds; key 856, the same twice. The lead set <code>REAL_SHARE = .5</code>, <code>FIND_LATE = 0</code>. <code>machine_check</code> 5o: 53% repairs, 5.4 jobs a shift day, faults from Day 2, ship health 95+. <code>FIND_LATE</code> is not used.</p>
  <h3>Who walks you to the first puzzle? (seats H and C, through the lead, 2026-10-18)</h3>
  <p>P3 is a <b>small</b> ladder row, and only big rows get a teacher's escort. So <b>P3 borrows the scanner lesson's escort</b>: the scanner lesson's second machine (yours) is <code>4-washer-2</code>, the escort walks you there, and it doesn't end at the reading. The P3 lesson starts on the spot and the escort carries on until the case is called. For this first case only, the first note comes with the job card (handed over at the washer) and the second is on the washer's panel. Later P3 cases follow the normal rule (the first note at the board, the others at the machine).</p>
  <h3>Day 5's escort, as built (seat C, 0.44.6)</h3>
  <p><code>worksEscortHold('m7')</code> (called by 24g and 24k while the scanner lesson runs) keeps the teacher with you, following, after your training job is turned in. <code>m7</code> counts as trained only on <code>worksEscortEnd('m7')</code> or <code>simNote('caseCalled', { train: true })</code>. Your training job must be the P3 machine itself: <b><code>4-washer-2</code></b>, or <code>4-washer-3</code> if Washer 2 is busy with your own wash (pass it to <code>jobReserve</code> by id; never "whatever it picks"), so the reading and the case are on the same washer.</p>
  <h3>Where your tools live (seat C)</h3>
  <p>In your kit locker in the Works bay, not at your bunk (the wardrobe there is the clothes section's).</p>
</section>
